WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.570
In this video, you describe an image, a network and a virtual machine as code with the Harvester Terraform provider, create them in SUSE Virtualization, change the virtual machine with a plan, and remove everything again.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:22.750
main.tf pins the Harvester provider and points it at a kubeconfig. Here it belongs to an account that can only use the demo namespace.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:29.740
The first resource is an image. The cluster downloads the openSUSE Leap cloud image from this address.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:36.480
The second is a VLAN network, VLAN one hundred on the management cluster network, with its subnet and gateway.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:46.620
The virtual machine uses both. One CPU, two gigabytes of memory, a ten gigabyte disk from the image, a NIC on the network, and cloud-init user data.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:52.840
terraform init downloads the provider version that main.tf asks for.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:58.910
terraform apply first computes a plan. Each plus sign is an attribute of a resource that does not exist yet.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:06.210
The plan adds three resources. Terraform creates the network and the image first, then the virtual machine that needs them.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:13.340
In the UI, demo-tf-vm is running with one CPU and two gigabytes of memory, just as main.tf describes it.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:19.630
The Virtual Machine Networks page lists demo-tf-net, the VLAN network with VLAN ID one hundred.

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:26.900
Now change the code. In main.tf, the CPU count goes from one to two, and the memory from two to four gigabytes.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:34.920
terraform plan compares the code with the cluster. The tilde marks an in-place update of the virtual machine: CPU and memory.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:40.470
Nothing is added or destroyed. One resource changes.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:48.280
terraform apply makes the change. Because restart_after_update is set, the provider restarts the virtual machine so the new size takes effect.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:57.840
In the UI, the details of demo-tf-vm now show two virtual CPUs and four gigabytes of memory, after the restart.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:05.850
When you no longer need it, terraform destroy removes the virtual machine, its disk, the network and the image.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:16.034
One file now describes the image, the network and the virtual machine, and Terraform keeps the cluster in line with it. Next, you bootstrap a Linux virtual machine with cloud-init.
